Both motions are away, so \(v_L=-12\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\) and \(v_S=-18\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\):
\[
f_L=450\frac{340-12}{340-(-18)}
=412\,\mathrm{Hz}
\]

For a source moving toward a stationary listener,
\[
f_L=f_S\frac{v}{v-v_S}
\]
Rearrange:
\[
v-v_S=\frac{f_Sv}{f_L}
\]
\[
v_S=v-\frac{f_Sv}{f_L}
=340-\frac{(800)(340)}{880}
=30.9\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}
\]

A stationary source emits wavelength
\[
\lambda=\frac{v}{f_S}
\]
A listener moving toward the source meets wavefronts at relative speed \(v+v_L\). Therefore
\[
f_L=\frac{v+v_L}{\lambda}
=\frac{v+v_L}{v/f_S}
=f_S\frac{v+v_L}{v}
\]
